.TH ADSMTC 1 "2026-06-30" "adsmt 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
adsmtc \- lu\-kb\-successor verification compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B adsmtc
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B adsmtc
is the batch
.I compiler
half of the lu\-kb\-successor CLI trichotomy
.RB ( lu\-smt (1)
is the SMT\-LIB driver;
.BR adsmtr (1)
is the interactive runtime). It reads a lu\-kb\-successor program (a
.B .lukb
file, or standard input when
.I FILE
is omitted), elaborates it, lowers it to the adsmt\-core kernel, runs the
unified solve over each obligation, and prints the
.B UnifiedVerdict
on standard output.

A verification obligation
.RB ( H " \(rh " G )
is discharged exactly when
.RB ( H " \(an \(no" G )
is unsatisfiable (the Verus convention), so a
.I verified
obligation reads
.BR unsat .
The verdict is a separated product of an SMT side and an ASP side that
collapses to a tri\-state by 3\-valued Kleene conjunction (an
.B unsat
on either side dominates the whole).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-output\-mode \fIz3\fR | \fIfull\fR
Select the verdict presentation.
.B z3
(the default) prints the collapsed verdict
.RB ( sat ", " unsat ", " unknown );
.B full
un\-collapses it to the 5\-level SMT precision lattice
.RB ( definite\-sat ", " possibly\-sat ", " unknown ", " possibly\-unsat ", " definite\-unsat )
and, for an ASP obligation, the 3\-valued well\-founded model.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status follows the obligation convention (it is the
.I inverse
of an ordinary SMT solver: a discharged proof obligation is
.BR unsat ):
.TP
.B 1
.B unsat
\(em the obligations were discharged
.RB ( VERIFIED ).
.TP
.B 0
.B sat
\(em a counterexample / model exists (the goal is NOT valid).
.TP
.B 2
.B unknown
\(em the engine could not decide.
.TP
.B 3
Usage or I/O error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lu\-smt (1),
.BR adsmtr (1)
.SH AUTHOR
NEWSNIPER.
